WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.628
Subprocesses should run with the least privilege needed for the approved
task.

2
00:00:04.628 --> 00:00:10.568
They should not run as administrator or root-equivalent accounts simply
because that is convenient.

3
00:00:10.568 --> 00:00:24.176
Service accounts, workload identities, containers, restricted runtimes,
sandboxing concepts, read-only filesystems, constrained volumes, and
limited network access can reduce damage if a child process behaves
unexpectedly.

4
00:00:24.176 --> 00:00:28.182
The exact mechanism changes by platform, but the principle does not.

5
00:00:28.182 --> 00:00:31.497
Logging should support review without leaking secrets.

6
00:00:31.497 --> 00:00:43.171
Useful records include the approved operation name, component,
environment, caller context at an appropriate level, correlation ID,
duration, timeout status, exit category, and safe reason code.

7
00:00:43.171 --> 00:00:52.565
Logs should avoid raw sensitive arguments, credentials, tokens, private
file contents, full environment dumps, full internal output, and
unnecessary paths.

8
00:00:52.565 --> 00:00:56.156
High-impact subprocess activity may also need audit events.

9
00:00:56.156 --> 00:00:59.334
Testing should include more than the successful path.

10
00:00:59.334 --> 00:01:12.389
Tests can verify approved operations, rejected inputs, shell avoidance,
path containment, environment minimization, timeout behavior, output
limits, error translation, authorization checks, and logging redaction.

11
00:01:12.389 --> 00:01:21.023
Code review should ask who can influence the executable, arguments,
working directory, files, environment, streams, privileges, and lifecycle.

12
00:01:21.023 --> 00:01:23.924
Those questions expose hidden trust boundaries.

13
00:01:23.924 --> 00:01:37.669
Monitoring should watch for unusual subprocess behavior: repeated
failures, rejected attempts, unexpected operation names, long-running
jobs, timeouts, high concurrency, large output, or execution from
unexpected components.

14
00:01:37.669 --> 00:01:48.859
If unsafe behavior or leaked output is discovered, teams should contain
the workflow, rotate exposed secrets when needed, review logs and
artifacts, fix the design, and add regression tests.

15
00:01:48.859 --> 00:01:52.520
Secure subprocess usage must be maintained as systems evolve.
